    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "nums = [7, 2, 5, 10, 8], k = 2  ->  18   ([7, 2, 5] and [10, 8])\nnums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5], k = 2   ->  9    ([1, 2, 3] and [4, 5])\nnums = [1, 4, 4], k = 3         ->  4"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The idea: \"minimize the largest\" means search the limit"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Fix a **limit** and ask: can the array be cut into at most `k` pieces, none summing past the limit? Greedy answers it — extend the current piece while it stays within the limit, otherwise start a new one. Fewer than k pieces is fine too, because any piece can be split further without raising the largest sum. A higher limit never needs more pieces, so the answers flip from no to yes once. The limit is at least the **biggest value** (it has to fit in some piece) and at most the **total**. This is exactly Capacity To Ship Packages, with pieces for days."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. Dynamic programming"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "`best[j][i]` is the smallest largest-sum when the first `i` values are cut into `j` pieces. The last piece is `nums[p..i−1]` for some `p`, so take the best over all `p` of the larger of `best[j−1][p]` and that piece's sum (from prefix sums)."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function splitArray(nums, k) {\n  const n = nums.length;\n  const prefix = [0];\n  for (const x of nums) prefix.push(prefix[prefix.length - 1] + x);\n  const best = Array.from({ length: k + 1 }, () => Array(n + 1).fill(Infinity));\n  best[0][0] = 0;                                  // zero values in zero pieces\n  for (let j = 1; j <= k; j++) {\n    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n      for (let p = j - 1; p < i; p++) {            // the last piece is nums[p..i-1]\n        best[j][i] = Math.min(best[j][i], Math.max(best[j - 1][p], prefix[i] - prefix[p]));\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  return best[k][n];\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def split_array(nums, k):\n    n = len(nums)\n    prefix = [0]\n    for x in nums:\n        prefix.append(prefix[-1] + x)\n    INF = float(\"inf\")\n    best = [[INF] * (n + 1) for _ in range(k + 1)]\n    best[0][0] = 0                        # zero values in zero pieces\n    for j in range(1, k + 1):\n        for i in range(1, n + 1):\n            for p in range(j - 1, i):     # the last piece is nums[p:i]\n                best[j][i] = min(best[j][i], max(best[j - 1][p], prefix[i] - prefix[p]))\n    return best[k][n]"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int splitArray(int[] nums, int k) {\n    int n = nums.length;\n    long[] prefix = new long[n + 1];\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + nums[i];\n    long[][] best = new long[k + 1][n + 1];\n    for (long[] row : best) Arrays.fill(row, Long.MAX_VALUE);\n    best[0][0] = 0;                               // zero values in zero pieces\n    for (int j = 1; j <= k; j++) {\n        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n            for (int p = j - 1; p < i; p++) {     // the last piece is nums[p..i-1]\n                best[j][i] = Math.min(best[j][i], Math.max(best[j - 1][p], prefix[i] - prefix[p]));\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return (int) best[k][n];\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int splitArray(vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n    int n = nums.size();\n    vector<long long> prefix(n + 1, 0);\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + nums[i];\n    vector<vector<long long>> best(k + 1, vector<long long>(n + 1, LLONG_MAX));\n    best[0][0] = 0;                               // zero values in zero pieces\n    for (int j = 1; j <= k; j++) {\n        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n            for (int p = j - 1; p < i; p++) {     // the last piece is nums[p..i-1]\n                best[j][i] = min(best[j][i], max(best[j - 1][p], prefix[i] - prefix[p]));\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return (int)best[k][n];\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(k · n²), space O(k · n).** Correct and a classic DP, but slow for n = 1000 and k = 50."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. Binary search on the limit"
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "split-array",
      "caption": "At each limit, cut the array greedily and count the pieces; at most k means the limit works."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function splitArray(nums, k) {\n  const pieces = (limit) => {                      // pieces needed if none may sum past limit\n    let count = 1, sum = 0;\n    for (const x of nums) {\n      if (sum + x > limit) { count++; sum = 0; }   // start a new piece\n      sum += x;\n    }\n    return count;\n  };\n  let low = Math.max(...nums);                     // a piece holds at least the biggest value\n  let high = nums.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);      // one piece holds everything\n  while (low < high) {\n    const mid = low + Math.floor((high - low) / 2);\n    if (pieces(mid) <= k) high = mid;              // possible: try a smaller limit\n    else low = mid + 1;                            // needs more than k pieces: raise the limit\n  }\n  return low;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def split_array(nums, k):\n    def pieces(limit):                    # pieces needed if none may sum past limit\n        count, total = 1, 0\n        for x in nums:\n            if total + x > limit:\n                count, total = count + 1, 0   # start a new piece\n            total += x\n        return count\n    low, high = max(nums), sum(nums)      # the biggest value .. everything in one piece\n    while low < high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n        if pieces(mid) <= k:\n            high = mid                    # possible: try a smaller limit\n        else:\n            low = mid + 1                 # needs more than k pieces: raise the limit\n    return low"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int splitArray(int[] nums, int k) {\n    long low = 0, high = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) { low = Math.max(low, x); high += x; }   // biggest value .. total\n    while (low < high) {\n        long mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        if (pieces(nums, mid) <= k) high = mid;   // possible: try a smaller limit\n        else low = mid + 1;                       // needs more than k pieces: raise the limit\n    }\n    return (int) low;\n}\n\nint pieces(int[] nums, long limit) {             // pieces needed if none may sum past limit\n    int count = 1;\n    long sum = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        if (sum + x > limit) { count++; sum = 0; }   // start a new piece\n        sum += x;\n    }\n    return count;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int pieces(vector<int>& nums, long long limit) { // pieces needed if none may sum past limit\n    int count = 1;\n    long long sum = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        if (sum + x > limit) { count++; sum = 0; }   // start a new piece\n        sum += x;\n    }\n    return count;\n}\n\nint splitArray(vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n    long long low = *max_element(nums.begin(), nums.end());\n    long long high = accumulate(nums.begin(), nums.end(), 0LL);   // biggest value .. total\n    while (low < high) {\n        long long mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        if (pieces(nums, mid) <= k) high = mid;   // possible: try a smaller limit\n        else low = mid + 1;                       // needs more than k pieces: raise the limit\n    }\n    return (int)low;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n log(total)), space O(1).** The search finds the smallest limit that works, and that limit is always an actual piece sum: if no piece hit it exactly, a smaller limit would work too."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Comparing the approaches"
    },
    {
      "type": "complexity",
      "rows": [
        {
          "approach": "Dynamic programming",
          "time": "O(k · n²)",
          "timeWhy": "**k · n** states, each trying up to **n** places for the last cut.",
          "space": "O(k · n)",
          "spaceWhy": "The **k × n** table."
        },
        {
          "approach": "Binary search on the limit",
          "time": "O(n log(total))",
          "timeWhy": "About **log(total)** limits, each checked greedily in **O(n)**.",
          "space": "O(1)",
          "spaceWhy": "The bounds and one pass's counters."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Edge cases to test"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**k = 1** — the total.",
        "**k = n** — every value alone: the biggest value.",
        "**Zeros** — `[0, 0, 5]` with k = 2: 5.",
        "**Large sums** — the total can pass 2³¹; Java and C++ use 64-bit sums."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Variations"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Capacity To Ship Packages Within D Days** — the same problem in different words.",
        "**Painter's Partition** and **Book Allocation** — classic names for the same search.",
        "**Divide Chocolate** — maximize the smallest piece instead: a last-yes search."
      ]
    }
